<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="biostats-act-15" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>🏫 Activity 15 - Final Project Workday</title>

    <introduction>
        <p> This assignment is due at the beginning of class on <alert> Wednesday, November 11</alert>. It is graded based on completeness according to the rubric available on Canvas. You are encouraged to use your notes and collaborate with your group members to complete this activity. </p>

        <p> <alert> This activity contains TWO parts of your final project. </alert> Extra care should be taken in completing this activity. </p>
    </introduction>

<page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <alert> This problem is a portion of your final project </alert> and will involve heavier use of RStudio. Please save all of the code for this problem in an R script named <c>02_LASTNAME_FIRSTNAME_Activity15_Independence.r</c>. When you are finished with the problem, save your changes and submit the R script to the corresponding Activity 15 assignment in Canvas. If you are the group leader, you will also submit a copy of the written report for this section to the assignment on Canvas called "Test for Independence Check-in". 
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Goal:</alert> In this portion of the final project, you will design a test for independence on two qualitative variables from your data set.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Select the two qualitative variables in your data set that you suspect are or are not dependent. I recommend first considering variables with small event space (few outcomes). Write this hypothesis as a comment at the top of your R script. 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    You can write comments in R by starting a line with a #.
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Extract the two variables of interest and remove any missing data from them. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the <c>chisq.test</c> command to perform a test for independence between the two variables. 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <hint>
                <p>
                    There are many several different ways you can use the <c>chisq.test</c> command to accomplish this goal. I am not picky about which you use. 
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Summarize this information in a written report. Your report should speak to each of the following prompts. Your answer should be a narrative, not a bulleted list of answers. 
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Clearly state your group's null and alternative hypotheses in both words and symbols. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe the process by which you isolated and cleaned your variables of interest.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Test your hypothesis with 95% confidence. Clearly distinguish between your test statistic and critical value. Explain how you used RStudio to compute obtain these statistics.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Conclude whether to reject your null hypothesis and cite your computed statistics as evidence.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Interpret the results from your test in the context of the problem using plain language.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>   
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <alert> This problem is a portion of your final project </alert> and will involve heavier use of RStudio. Please save all of the code for this problem in an R script named <c>02_LASTNAME_FIRSTNAME_Activity15_anova.r</c>. When you are finished with the problem, save your changes and submit the R script to the corresponding Activity 15 assignment in Canvas. If you are the group leader, you will also submit a copy of the written report for this section to the assignment on Canvas called "One-Way AnOVa Check-in". 
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Goal:</alert> In this portion of the final project, you will design a One-Way AnOVa test to determine whether a treatment causes significant change in a measured mean from each group.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Identify your variables of interest. One variable will define your treatment groups, and one variable will define the measurements you need to take.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    I would strongly recommend that you choose a qualitative variable with few outcomes to define your treatment groups. 
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Remove all samples that are missing data for either of your variables.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the <c>as.factor()</c> command to label your treatment variable as a <c>factor</c> object. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Separate the remaining data into treatment groups. 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <hint>
                <p>
                    There are several ways you might go about doing this. Consider the <c>dplyr::filter(), table()</c>, or <c>group_by</c> commands for inspiration.
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute the sample size, mean, and standard deviation of the outcome variable in each treatment group. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Apply the <c>aov</c> command to your data set and use the output to construct a One-Way AnOVa table for your data.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</page>
